suit alleging wrongdoing  based on affidavits from republican poll  watchers  including claims that the workers were  not able to properly view ballot  counting due to covet 19 restrictions  one man says he suspected fraud with  military ballots  because he was doubtful that they would  have voted for joe biden so a lot of  this is public relations  the remedies they seek stop everything  or hold another election that's not  going to happen  and what about the ever popular claim  that thousands of dead people  voted it hasn't panned out experts  acknowledge a small number of  irregularities are possible  are we talking about half a dozen are we  talking about a dozen are we talking  about 20  these are incredibly ordinary uh sorts  of problems to have with any large-scale  electoral system  none of it would change the outcome of  the election in trump's favor  but that may not be the end goal a new  poll finds eight in ten republicans now  believe  biden's win is illegitimate as the  president and his allies work to  manufacture doubt  and that could leave the president-elect  facing a very steep  uphill battle on day one 70 days from  now  donna jackson thanks  starry salute still ahead and out of  this world tribute to the fallen in the  uk  in london only a select few attended an  armistice day service in westminster  abbey  because of the pandemic the public was  discouraged from gathering its cenotaphs  but as crystal gomencing explains people  still found a way to pay tribute to the  fallen  by looking to the stars the twinkling  lights  beckon you to click the stars are  stories  some are brief sharing only a name  others  include service records you can just  name a star after them and it appears in  this virtual universe that we've created  by writing the names of veterans in the  sky the commonwealth war graves  commission  hopes to educate and connect people  peter francis was able to accompany two  veterans an  88 year old and an 81 year old on a  virtual  starry stroll and the whoops of joy i  got on the other end of the line when  they saw the start  absolutely made my heart warm and it  just made  all of the hard work behind the campaign  worthwhile  this year people were not only  discouraged from gathering in big groups  at cenotaphs  many weren't able to even pin on a poppy  instead britons were encouraged to  donate online  and print one out this year we haven't  set a target because  the the environment is just so different  but what we do know is that since the  lockdown  since we've had to cancel the  face-to-face collections for the first  time 100 years  we know that we will be taking a loss in  the in the realm of millions  [Music]  inside westminster abbey only a handful  of people were permitted to attend a  service at the grave of the unknown  warrior  with england in the middle of another  lockdown the abbey is closed to visitors  [Applause]  many had hoped this armistice day would  be the in-person  gathering the nation was deprived of in  may  victory in europe celebrations were  cancelled because of the first lockdown  still people are trying to honor all of  those who have served  and sacrificed we're not letting it pass  away that torch of remembrance is still  burning brightly and strongly  burning and lighting the way to explore  the cosmos  and learn the name of the stars crystal  gomansing global news  london next honoring canada's vulnerable  veterans  remembrance day ceremonies are usually a  time for veterans to gather together  share stories and be honored by the  public as mike derolay reports this  year's scale back events meant a very  different  experience for our vets alex paulowin  can't help but feel lonely  he's the last of the brave crew of the  hmcs huron a destroyer that helped clear  the english channel ahead of d-day  every remembrance day he's reminded of  that fact  i think i'm about the only man left  standing on the last ship that i was on  you know it's it's a sad feeling you  know that  knowing that they're all gone the  reminders of his generation disappearing  are  far too frequent last week bc's george  chow  who fought both racism at home and the  nazis in europe  died at the age of 99 they think about  their mortality  about the impact they made on this world  and with the pandemic forcing 375 vets  to stay inside  at sunnybrook hospital's veterans center  in toronto  they worry mistakes that were made could  be repeated  you have to have friends die in the lord  to realize what war is all about  unless you've had that experience it's  hard to  it's hard to believe what the  significance of wars  are the drone of four canadian harvard's  cut the silence of a scaled back  remembrance day in toronto  from coast to coast to coast canadians  were reluctantly urged to stay away  [Music]  that led to a smallish crowd in halifax  and so few people in montreal you might  miss it if you drove past  virtual broadcasts like this one in  winnipeg for all their good intentions  were heartbreaking to see and while the  vets at sunnybrook could only look out  their windows  their view was a glorious sea of  canadian flags  100 for each of the 375 still there  thirty seven thousand five hundred in  total one veteran told me a story about  how his best friend from childhood had  gone off to war with him  and his best friend had been killed in  battle  and he pointed out to the flags on our  lawn and said but now he's here with us  it wasn't the ceremony they deserved but  at least it reminded them  we haven't forgotten microlight global  news toronto  and that is global national for this  wednesday remembrance day i'm donna  friesen  we leave you tonight with more tributes  to canadians who have served and  sacrificed for our country  thanks for watching hope to see you here  again tomorrow take care of yourselves  we're called the caring  canadians i think  that's a wonderful legacy  to have  [Music]  [Music]  sorry
 

